**The bus factor is about 2 by line count. The original author, Felix "xq" Queißner, still wrote about 35% of the current code.**

I ran the busfactor script with `--dry-run --no-github`, so nothing was written to the repo. I also ran `git blame` over the 2,120 lines of `.zig` source. The history is 132 commits, from 2020-03-04 to 2026-11-04.

**If xq stopped answering**
- xq committed under four emails: `mq32.de`, `masterq32.de`, `ib-queissner.de` and `random-projects.net`. Merged, they account for about 751 of 2,120 lines (35%).
- That code would have no author left to ask. It is the original foundation and not tied to one subsystem.
- xq is not fully gone from the project. The last commits were on 2026-04-16, so they count as active under the tool's 12-month window.
- The tool also lists one of xq's emails (`<email redacted>`) as part of the repo-wide fallback owners. That fallback is where review requests would land.

**Who else holds the code**

| Author | Lines | Share |
|---|---|---|
| Wen Li | 552 | 26% |
| xq (merged) | 751 | 35% |
| jnagpaul | 188 | 9% |
| Vincent Rischmann | 130 | 6% |
| mochalins | 77 | 4% |
| everyone else | the remaining ~20% | |

- **Bus factor 2:** xq alone is under half the code, but xq plus Wen Li is about 61%. Losing both would leave most of the code without an author.
- **Concentration in the last month:** Wen Li and Tobias Ek made 6 commits in the last month (since 2026-10-06). All of Wen Li's commits are in that window, and the tool names Wen Li and Tobias as the `/src/` owners.
- **Fresh, not proven:** so most of the current ownership signal is a few weeks old. That is a thin basis for calling anyone the lasting owner.
- **Already gone:** jnagpaul's last commit was in July 2023 and they have only 7 commits, so their 188 lines (about 9%) are effectively orphaned. Other one-time contributors add a bit more.
- **Ownership split:** the rest of the code is spread across many one-off contributors, so no single other person is a risk.

**Caveats**
- Line blame measures who wrote the code, not who understands it. Reviewers and maintainers who rarely commit don't show up.
- I didn't write `.github/CODEOWNERS` or `ownership.json` and didn't check whether anyone marked inactive is still around. Say so if you want me to run it for real.
- The tool produced a very short CODEOWNERS (a fallback plus one `/src/` rule), so it found no meaningful per-directory split.

